<section id="play">
  <h2>The turn / scene loop</h2><p class="source-note">Pages 20–21</p>
  <div class="flow"><div><strong>Reveal</strong><br />Place the top card beside the deck on the Narrative Track.</div><div><strong>Interpret</strong><br />Use the scenario’s matching prompts, or invent something suitable.</div><div><strong>Resolve</strong><br />If it is an obstacle, roll a d10 and optionally spend Resolve.</div><div><strong>Narrate</strong><br />Write the scene in your journal or describe it aloud.</div><div><strong>Continue</strong><br />Draw the next card and connect the new scene to the previous one.</div></div>
  <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:14px"><div class="card"><h3>Group play</h3><p>The Active Narrator draws, interprets, rolls, spends Resolve, and has final say. Other players may suggest ideas. Pass the role to the player on the left after each scene.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Time in the fiction</h3><p>Every <strong>two cards</strong> represent one day in the journal. The final Grey Lady represents a whole day by itself.</p></div></div>
</section>

<section id="obstacles">
  <h2>Overcoming obstacles</h2><p class="source-note">Page 21</p>
  <div class="grid-2"><div class="rule-box"><h3>The roll</h3><div class="equation">Roll d10 ≥ card number + Grey Ladies already revealed</div><p><strong>Success:</strong> the protagonist overcomes the obstacle and gains a beneficial outcome.</p><p><strong>Failure:</strong> the protagonist is hindered and loses <span class="token spirit">1 Spirit</span>.</p><p class="note">Failure creates a fresh problem; it does not stop the story dead.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="label">Spend Resolve</span><p>Spend any number of Resolve points, but choose <strong>one timing only</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Before rolling:</strong> each Resolve gives <strong>+2</strong>.</li><li><strong>After rolling:</strong> each Resolve gives <strong>+1</strong>.</li></ul><p class="danger">You cannot spend Resolve both before and after the same roll.</p></div></div>
  <div class="card" style="margin-top:14px"><h3>Example</h3><p>You reveal an Environment Obstructs <strong>6</strong>. Two Grey Ladies are already on the Narrative Track, so the target is <strong>8</strong>. You may spend 1 Resolve before rolling for +2, or roll first and spend Resolve afterward for +1 each.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="grey-ladies">
  <h2>When a Grey Lady appears</h2><p class="source-note">Pages 19 and 22</p>
  <div class="grid-3"><div class="card"><span class="big-number">1</span><h3>Escalate the fiction</h3><p>Use the next entry on the scenario’s Tension Table, in order.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="big-number">2</span><h3>Pay the cost</h3><p>Either spend <span class="token resolve">1 Resolve</span> or lose <span class="token spirit">1 Spirit</span>.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="big-number">3</span><h3>Raise all future targets</h3><p>Every Grey Lady on the Narrative Track adds <strong>+1</strong> to obstacle target numbers.</p></div></div>
  <p class="note">At 0 Spirit, you remain at 0. If a Grey Lady appears and you cannot lose more Spirit, spend Resolve if any remains.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Useful edge cases</h2><p class="source-note">Page 21</p>
  <div class="grid-2"><div class="card"><h3>Duplicate card in a row</h3><p>You may play it normally. Or hold the duplicate, draw another card now, and play the held card on the next turn.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Not inspired by the scenario prompt?</h3><p>The listed story elements are suggestions. Invent your own scene, provided it fits the card type and grows from the existing tale.</p></div></div>
</section>
